 for bestpractices and # AI Assistant & Agent Layer

## Overview
Develop a robust, multi-tenant AI assistant and agent layer that combines retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), role-aware prompt composition, prompt templates, source citations, and secure action triggering with permission checks. This feature powers an AI Workspace constrained to tenant data, supports streaming outputs, maintains full conversation and action audit logs, and exposes a Global Dashboard for high-level governance. Integrate with an OpenAI-compatible LLM provider for conversation, summarization, and content generation. Ensure strict runtime safety (null checks, proper typing, and auditability) and enforce authentication/authorization before data access or action execution.

---

## Components to Build
1) AI Assistant & Agent Layer (Core)
- Role-aware context engine: computes user role, tenant, workspace, and allowed data scope.
- RAG pipeline: structured and unstructured tenant data ingestion with a vector store; support for both document/context retrieval and knowledge base queries.
- Prompt templating system: per-department/use-case templates with dynamic slot filling (tenant, role, data sources, action permissions).
- Source citations: attach provenance (data source, document reference, timestamp) to outputs where applicable.
- Action triggering with permission checks: triggerable actions gated by role-based permissions; require confirmation for sensitive steps.
- Streaming response support: support token-by-token streaming from the LLM with proper backpressure and progress events.
- Conversation history & audit: store all prompts, responses, and actions with timestamps and actor metadata.
- Telemetry & usage accounting: track token usage, response times, and model/version metadata per tenant.

2) API Layer (OpenAI / LLM Provider)
- LLM abstraction: a provider-compatible interface (generate, stream, summarize) with pluggable providers.
- Streaming handler: client-friendly streaming response (onChunk, onEnd) with proper backpressure and error handling.
- Token accounting: expose per-tenant token usage counters; provide hooks for billing integration.
- Safety wrappers: enforce content safety constraints, rate limits, and fail gracefully.

3) Global Dashboard (Frontend)
- Role-aware landing dashboard: aggregated KPIs, alerts, recent activity, and quick actions tailored by user role.
- Card/grid system: modular, responsive 12-column grid with hero metrics and dashboards tailored per tenant/role.
- AI widgets: quick-glance AI insights, status of AI actions, and AI activity feed with citations and provenance.
- Notification center: alerts about AI usage, permission denials, and action requirements.
- Theming: implement the provided visual design system and typography guidelines.

4) AI Workspace (Frontend)
- Conversational AI interface: chat-style UI with modes Ask, Analyze, Report, Action.
- Context scope: tenant data bound; data visibility restricted by role.
- Prompt palette: UI to select/define prompt templates and supply case-specific parameters.
- Conversation history: persistent thread with per-message metadata and ability to re-query.
- Source citations UX: show source references alongside AI responses where available.
- Action panel: show allowed actions; require user confirmation when executing actions.
- Studio-like controls: per-tenant AI context configuration (data sources, allowed actions, templates).

5) Data & Backend Models
- Tenant-scoped data models: users, roles, permissions, tenants, workspaces, AI templates, prompts, actions, and audit logs.
- Unified context store: normalized entities (Company, Department, User, Document, Conversation, Action, AuditLog, KPI, Task, etc.).
- Vector store integration: support for embeddings with tenant scoping and metadata.
- Audit logs: immutable-like storage with read-access controls.

6) Integration & Security
- AuthN/AuthZ: enforce tenant isolation and RBAC-based access controls across all endpoints.
- Permissions model: granular permissions for data access, AI usage, and action triggers.
- Data isolation: ensure that tenant data never leaks across tenants.
- Validation layer: robust input validation and API response shape checks.

---

## Implementation Requirements

### Frontend
- Pages:
  - Global Dashboard (page_global_dashboard_008)
  - AI Workspace (page_ai_workspace_013)
- Components:
  - Card, KPI tile, Chart area, Activity feed, Alerts panel
  - AI chat panel with streaming support UI
  - Templates selector and parameter form
  - Action confirmation modal with permission checks
  - Source citations list and per-output provenance badges
  - Role-aware quick action bar
- Data handling:
  - Use React with TypeScript
  - State management (useState, useReducer, or a lightweight store)
  - Initialize array states with proper defaults: useState<Type[]>([])
  - Guard all array operations: (items ?? []).map(...) or Array.isArray(items) ? items.map(...) : []
  - Ensure null-safety for any API results: const dataList = Array.isArray(res?.data) ? res.data : []
- Accessibility:
  - Keyboard navigable, ARIA labels for dialogs and inputs
- Theming & typography:
  - Implement the given design system colors, typography, spacing, and grid (12-column responsive)
  - Ensure hero and card layouts match spacing guidelines
- UX:
  - Streaming chat UX with progressive rendering
  - Show live status indicators for AI actions (neon teal status dots)
  - Show citations alongside responses with expandable/collapsible source details

### Backend
- Models (illustrative):
  - Tenant, User, Role, Permission
  - AI_Template, PromptTemplate, PromptInstance
  - Conversation, Message, SourceCitation
  - Action, ActionLog, PermissionCheckpoint
  - ContextDocument, ContextRecord, VectorNote
  - AuditLog, KPI, Alert
- APIs:
  - POST /ai/conversations: initiate or continue a chat session
  - GET /ai/conversations/:id: fetch conversation history
  - POST /ai/prompts/assemble: build a prompt from template and context
  - POST /ai/actions/execute: perform an action after permission checks
  - GET /ai/actions/permissions: fetch permitted actions for current user
  - POST /ai/stream: streaming response endpoint
  - GET /dashboard/summary: fetch KPIs, alerts, activity
  - GET /contexts/:tenant/:workspace: fetch tenant-scoped data for RAG
- Data handling:
  - All array-type results must be guarded: (data ?? []) etc.
  - Validate API responses: const list = Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : []
  - Use null-safe property access throughout
- Vector store integration:
  - Persist embeddings for structured/unstructured tenant data
  - Query by tenant and workspace
- Auditing:
  - Persist for every AI action and user-initiated operation
  - Include actor, timestamp, action details, and outcome

### Integration
- Data flow:
  - Frontend authenticates; backend resolves tenant, role, workspace
  - Retrieve context (structured + unstructured) from unified data layer and vector store
  - Assemble prompt via PromptTemplate with role- and context-aware parameters
  - Send to OpenAI provider; stream tokens back to frontend
  - If action requested, perform permission checks and request confirmation if needed
  - Log conversation and actions in AuditLog
- Security:
  - Enforce tenant isolation at every boundary
  - Validate inputs and outputs; handle model failures gracefully
  - Ensure streaming responses include error signaling

---

## User Experience Flow

1) User authenticates and lands on Global Dashboard
- Sees KPIs, alerts, and recent activity tailored to their role.
- Quick actions reflect their permissions (e.g., “Run AI Analysis”, “Request Report”).

2) User navigates to AI Workspace
- Chooses a mode: Ask, Analyze, Report, Action
- Context: tenant data bound; only allowed data is accessible per role
- User selects a Prompt Template (per department/use case) or builds a custom prompt
- User types a query or selects a task, system gathers context (documents, structured data, recent conversations)

3) AI generates response streaming
- Output shows partial responses as tokens arrive
- Citations are shown alongside outputs; details expandable
- If user asked for an action, UI presents a gated action panel

4) Action execution (if requested)
- System checks user permissions and, if required, prompts for confirmation
- Upon confirmation, action executes against the connected systems
- Action result is surfaced in UI and logged in AuditLog

5) Audit & history
- All conversations, prompts, responses, and actions are stored
- Users can review past AI interactions with citations and outcome status

6) Administration and governance
- Admins monitor AI usage, permissions, and integration status from the Global Dashboard

---

## Build Order & Dependencies (Mandatory)

- Prerequisites:
  - Authentication/Authorization system in place with RBAC
  - Core multi-tenant data layer and unified context store (Company, Department, User, Document, KPI, etc.)
  - OpenAI/L Liaison provider integration and configuration
  - Vector store (for RAG) and indexing pipelines for tenant data
  - Frontend scaffolding for Global Dashboard and AI Workspace pages

- Blocks:
  - Block 1: Data access controls + tenant isolation implemented
  - Block 2: RAG pipeline for tenant data; vector store indexing
  - Block 3: Prompt templating engine with role-aware composition
  - Block 4: Streaming LLM integration and token accounting
  - Block 5: AI Workspace UI with modes and citations
  - Block 6: Global Dashboard widgets with AI status and activity
  - Block 7: Action governance workflow (permissions + confirmation flows)
  - Block 8: Audit logging for conversations and actions
  - Block 9: End-to-end testing, integration tests, and performance benchmarks

- Sequencing Rule:
  Auth/access control and core data setup must be completed before dashboard/workspace/analytics tasks when both are present.

---

## Technical Specifications

- Data Models (high level):
  - Tenant(id, name, providerSettings, vectorStoreConfig, aiSettings)
  - User(id, tenantId, roles[], permissions[], profile)
  - Role(name, permissions[])
  - Permission(name, scope[])
  - ContextDocument(id, tenantId, source, content, metadata)
  - Conversation(id, tenantId, userId, mode, createdAt, messages[])
  - Message(id, conversationId, role, content, timestamp, citations[])
  - PromptTemplate(id, tenantId, department, purpose, templateText)
  - ActionLog(id, tenantId, userId, actionName, status, details, timestamp)
  - KPI(id, tenantId, metrics[], updatedAt)
  - Alert(id, tenantId, severity, message, timestamp)
  - ContextRecord(id, tenantId, type, data, source, linkedEntities[])

- API Endpoints (examples):
  - POST /api/ai/conversations
  - GET /api/ai/conversations/{id}
  - POST /api/ai/prompts/assemble
  - POST /api/ai/actions/execute
  - GET /api/ai/actions/permissions
  - POST /api/ai/stream
  - GET /api/dashboard/summary
  - GET /api/contexts/{tenantId}/{workspaceId}
  - POST /api/audit/log

- Security:
  - OAuth2 / JWT-based authentication
  - RBAC-based authorization per endpoint
  - Data access checks: tenantId, workspaceId, and role context

- Validation:
  - Use schema validation for requests; respond with well-formed error payloads
  - Null handling: always normalize to arrays when returning lists
  - Ensure all array operations guard against null/undefined

- Validation Rules:
  - All API responses that return lists must coerce: Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : []
  - Use data ?? [] for Supabase-like results
  - Destructure with defaults where applicable: const { items = [], count = 0 } = response ?? {}

---

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Multi-tenant isolation: data from one tenant is inaccessible to another; RBAC enforces access at all endpoints
- [ ] RAG retrieval returns relevant tenant-context documents and structured data, with citations and provenance
- [ ] Prompt templates render correctly per department/use case with role-aware constraints
- [ ] Streaming LLM responses render progressively without missing tokens; token usage accounting is accurate
- [ ] Actions are gated by permissions; confirmation is required for sensitive actions and audit logs reflect outcomes
- [ ] Conversation and action audit logs are stored reliably and can be queried by tenant, user, and timeframe
- [ ] Global Dashboard and AI Workspace UI render per design system, with responsive behavior and accessibility
- [ ] All code paths guard against null/undefined values before using array methods
- [ ] All API response shapes validated; lists default to [] when absent
- [ ] End-to-end test coverage for chat, prompt assembly, action triggering, and auditing

---

## UI/UX Guidelines
Apply the project's design system precisely.

- Color Palette: follow the provided deep navy backgrounds, pale sky blue accents, neon greens for live indicators, and muted grays for text
- Typography: use Poppins/Manrope for headings and Inter/Roboto for body; ensure hero displays align with 56–96px scales on desktop
- Layout: 12-column responsive grid with generous horizontal whitespace; hero-left text block and right-side cards in hero
- Cards: rounded 12–16px; very dark fill; subtle inner shadow; light border with low opacity
- Data Visuals: minimalistic, flat, precise; chart colors align with design tokens
- Interactions: smooth transitions (120–200ms), hover lifts, focus rings in pale blue
- Citations: per-output provenance visible; expandable details

